(type 7), \code{iqr = q3 - q1}, \code{mad} is \code{stats::mad()} (scaled by 1.4826),
\code{se = sd / sqrt(n)}, \code{ci} is the half-width of the t-based confidence
interval of the mean (\code{qt((1 + conf_level) / 2, n - 1) * se}), and
\code{ci_low} / \code{ci_high} are its bounds (\verb{mean -/+ ci}),
\code{cv = sd / mean} as a ratio (shown in percent with \code{fmt = "{cv:1\\\%}"}),
\code{skew} is the adjusted Fisher-Pearson skewness G1 and \code{kurt} the excess
kurtosis G2, as in SAS, SPSS and Excel's \code{SKEW()} / \code{KURT()} (\code{NA} with
fewer than 3 / 4 values).
The CV is only meaningful for strictly positive data and is \code{NA} when a
group contains values <= 0. Statistics that are undefined for a group
(e.g. \code{sd} with one value) are \code{NA}.

Computation: statistics are computed column by column on the input table
(no reshaping to long format, so memory use stays close to the input
size). \code{n}, \code{mean}, \code{sd}, \code{min}, \code{max} and \code{median} use data.table's
optimised grouped functions, quantiles are read off once-sorted groups,
so tens of thousands of groups (sires, litters, pens) are summarised in
about a second per million records.

Rows are ordered by variable (in the order of \code{cols}),
then by group (in the original order of the \code{by} values: numeric order,
factor levels, alphabetical for text), with the total row last. With a
total row, \code{by} columns that are not factors or character are returned as
character; factors gain the label as their last level.
